--- 5. θύρα   [KEEP]

  GREEK        κεκλεισμένης τῆς θύρας, ἔκρουσεν ὁ ξένος, καὶ οὐδεὶς ἤνοιξεν αὐτῷ.
  ENGLISH      The door having been shut, the stranger knocked, and no one opened for him.
  TARGET       θύρας

  REASONING    θύρα at 7.49, the door of a building as a physical, closable entrance. Anchor: it is shut, knocked upon, and someone stands outside it — an aperture, not a gate of a city or a metaphorical opportunity. A window or wall would not admit knocking and opening, so the swap test holds. Perfect passive participle plus a temporal ὅτε clause suits beyond_beginner.

  BLIND READ   Luke 13:25 (also echoed in Matthew 25:10-12, parable of the ten virgins / friend at midnight)
               A shut door, someone standing outside knocking, and no one opening for him is the exact shape of the 'exclusion from the house' scene used in Jesus' parables of judgment.

  VERDICT      keep -- The Greek is original and the sense/constraints are correctly handled, but the three-beat sequence of a shut door, a knock, and refusal closely tracks the exclusion scene in Luke 13:25/Matthew 25:10-12; this is worth flagging as a concern but does not by itself require revision.

  FINDINGS
    [echo/structure concern] The three-beat sequence — door shut, knocking from outside, no one opens — is the exact skeleton of the parable's exclusion scene, even though the vocabulary (κεκλεισμένης, ἔκρουσεν, ξένος, ἤνοιξεν) is the writer's own and not lifted wording.
    [echo/scene concern] A stranger shut out at a closed door is plausible as an everyday scene, but combined with the knock-and-refusal beats it reads as a quiet retelling of the judgment-door motif rather than an independent domestic scenario.
    [echo/theological rendering concern] The sentence carries the same charge of exclusion/refusal found in the parable, without adding anything beyond it — a bare compression of that beat rather than a fresh use of the item.

